 the list  exactly and even President Obama just  recently spoke out against sex  trafficking calling at modern-day  slavery he called it an outrage the  district state  apartment has devoted quite a bit of  resources to fighting against sex  trafficking and the public you know  consume products that feature sex  trafficking all the time I've been  thinking about the Liam Neeson movie  taken and I had been interested in about  the fight against sex trafficking and  where it had first started and i  discovered that sex trafficking is a  topic that has you know been around for  a very long time and in the early 20th  century it was called white slavery  people fought against it they organized  internationally they organized  nationally in the united states 45 out  of the 48 states passed local anti white  slavery laws in addition to the national  one that I study so one of the things  that I wanted wanted to think about was  at what this history can tell  contemporary activists I wanted to speak  to them and to alert them to the fact  that they are participating in a social  movement and a political movement as  deep historical roots additionally I  also wanted to think about how gendered  habits of mind informed the growth of  the American state so what I'm referring  to there is I'm thinking about how FBI  agents when they investigate this law  brought with them their own baggage  their own perceptions of the world about  what women's proper place should be and  is and how that shaped investigations  and shaped the growth of the FBI itself  very interesting how did you do your  research the research I did I did  research everywhere I traveled to  archives in Geneva and London and  Minneapolis Fort Worth Portland and all  points in between but the vast majority  of the of my records that I use the most  our house with the National Archives in  Washington DC mostly FBI records okay  well actually there's some really  interesting stories about that I use  about 1,000 a sample of 1,000  investigations out of there's many many  more there probably you know 60,000  investigations that were conducted  between 1910 and 1941 and one of the  things that I thought was very very  funny that I discovered while I was  doing a research is that in 1944 J Edgar  Hoover asked that these files be  examined to see if they can be turned  over to the National Archives because  the National Archives was pestering the  FBI to you know  hand over some records and when they  were examined the man examining them  said no we can't give them over there's  too much salacious detail there's too  much going on here he said that it would  be seriously embarrassing to the FBI it  would be politically suicidal for the  FBI to hand them over now of course they  did end up handing them over at a much  later date but it's the elements that  are kind of politically suicidal or very  salacious that I find to be really  really helpful in my in my work the FBI  investigated all types of families most  of these investigations never go to  court and that's the problem from the  FBI's perspective is that they're  investigating American families and kind  of who are going through personal  tragedies but there is no kind of  prosecutorial intent so what I find or j  edgar Hoover himself said that between  1921 and 1936 that the FBI had conducted  around 47,500 investigations yet during  the same period of time you only see  around 6300 court cases emerging out of  these so by looking at the  investigations rather than US Attorney's  court cases I'm able to get a much  broader view of what it is that be I was  doing in these investigations do you  think the FBI was improperly using the  Mann Act in in many instances since the  Mann Act was originally to combat  prostitution and it really veered off  into a whole nother realm that's a  really good question they I don't think  they were misusing it from their  standards in 1917 the Supreme Court  ruled on the white slave Traffic Act to  define its scope and in that decision it  said that the any other immoral purposes  Clause really truly meant any other  immoral purpose it could be pleasure  escapades it could be Affairs it could  be anything so the FBI was kind of  operating under you know from that  ruling the other really interesting part  of this is that for the vast majority of  these investigations particularly into  marital troubles and seduction cases  were initiated by the citizens involved  Hoover says in 1932 that seventy percent  of these cases were started because  somebody goes to the FBI office says hey  I need help my daughter has disappeared  or my wife has run off and so I don't  really look at this law as a  heavy-handed FBI top-down surveillance  law okay instead its citizens who are  begging for help and the FBI is there  and they have the jurisdiction to be  able to track people down and in many  ways the FBI doesn't really want to be  doing this I mean in 1939 they pull back  and say you know we can't we don't have  the resources we don't have the time we  don't have the budget to be getting  involved in that I am like quibbles  right investigate every wife who leaves  her husband goes across state line yeah  how does your work differ from much of  the other stuff that's out there that's  a great question most of the stuff  that's about white slavery examines the  popular culture artifacts the movies the  plays this was a very popular topic the  Reformers activism and most of it most  of the historians who do this work look  at white slavery and they look at it use  it as a lens to analyze a whole host of  anxieties fears of interracial sex fears  of immigration fears of women taking to  the streets demanding the right to vote  or women working in factories and so  they use it as a way to talk about other  cultural changes that are occurring  between nineteen hundred and nineteen  thirty and most of them do not look at  enforcement they don't they stop there  they look it up until the laws passed if  they look at enforcement at all they  look at a jack johnson case which is a  very celebrated case where the african  american boxer Jack Johnson he was  probably the world's most famous athlete  at the time really I'm not familiar with  this okay yeah and I prosecuted for what  for violating the white slave Traffic  Act because he had taken a girlfriend  who was a prostitute but they had a more  of a romantic relationship he had taken  her across state lines was she black her  was she was white okay so that was the  biggest that's the big issue and he was  convicted and he fled and you know left  the United States because he didn't want  to serve the time and traveled  throughout the world until eventually he  returned to the United States has served  his year in prison so most people talk  about the persecution of Jack Johnson as  a way  that this law was misused to uphold a  racial caste system that severely  punished non white men for having sexual  access to white women so when we talk  about enforcement that's the only case  that really gets brought up or perhaps  the prosecution of Charlie Chaplin and  other celebrities I'm looking at just  the regular cases where the they're more  run-of-the-mill cases I end up looking  at a completely different dynamic yes  interracial sex was very much policed  and I see that in my you know in my  evidence homosexual no no not really  which is interesting because in the  1920s the 1930s places like New York  City had a thriving you know kind of gay  scene and it's not really policed these  are very heterosexual Oh classes so  whoever didn't use the Mann Act really  that uh fascinating no and in some ways  the issue of homosexuality from the US  federal government isn't as present  prior to World War two I mean the  Immigration Bureau is keeping an eye and  enforcing kind of anti-gay policies at  the borders but you know the amount of  people that they're excluding is very  very small 