ecially on the law  enforcement side I conclude the  manuscript with this fascinating case  study of a 1936 sex trafficking ring  that operated here in New Haven the  people involved were trafficking young  girls up from New Jersey in New York to  places like Bridgeport New Haven  Hartford and all points in between when  we look at this case or when I look at  this case I find it to be incredibly  exploited it the girls involved had no  choice than types of customers they  serviced they had no control over the  wages that their work earned that their  prostitution earned they were an  incredibly abusive circumstances yet  this was incredibly profitable for the  people involved the FBI estimated that  this particular ring earned between 1.5  and 2 million dollars a year every  single year that's that's between about  24 and 32 million dollars near my  standards right so it's incredibly  profitable and the FBI's investigation  was able to to  conclude with 39 separate convictions of  separate people involved in this when  thinking about sex trafficking today and  this 1936 case I see a lot of  similarities and i think that there's  much to be learned by looking in the  past to think about what were the  contours of sex trafficking in the past  the contours of vice markets the  contours of prostitution how did it  happen and how did law enforcement try  to fight against it is the Mann Act  still in effect today it is there was  some talk when Eliot Spitzer got in all  of his trouble of going after him with  the Mann Act although that never really  developed it seems to be currently being  used against craigslist prostitution and  you know online prostitution I think  that it's going through a period of  revivalism within the Department of  Justice so certainly it's it's a tool  that the prosecutors now use okay thank  you very much for being here with us  today and sharing some of your work  thank you so much for more information  about professor ply lee and her research  please visit our website at yale edu  slash macmillan report be sure to join  us again for another episode of the  macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale

